2026-10-02.introduction:
Divers working in confined spaces, such as underwater cave systems [ 1 ] , pipelines [ 2 ] , coral reef canyons [ 3 ] , or around unexploded ordnance [ 4 ] , must often maintain a safe operating distance from the surrounding walls or objects.
In the case of cave diving, for example, avoiding cave walls helps divers maintain optimal visibility conditions by avoiding collisions with the surrounding enclosure.
This minimizes stirring up deposited sediment [ 5 ] , which is a leading cause of diver disorientation-induced fatalities in cave diving accidents [ 6 ] .
Maintaining safe standoff distances for divers during coral reef explorations is also important, since ocean currents, other divers, or marine obstacles can inadvertently cause divers to touch coral [ 3 ] , which devastates coral health.

In this paper, we introduce RADMCS ( R obotic A ssisted D iver M ovement in C onfined S paces) with the goal of addressing these distance-maintaining challenges.
RADMCS is an easy-to-deploy wearable robotic solution that assists in maintaining a preset standoff distance from encompassing walls or objects by providing force-feedback to the diver when they get too close to such objects.
The force-feedback mechanism is driven by monocular depth estimation techniques that estimate the distance between the robot’s cameras and the surrounding structure, and the robot uses its thrusters in response to this perceived distance.
Fig. 1 shows the haptic feedback mechanism working in response to the distance between the surrounding reef and the robot’s cameras.
Because the robot is affixed to the diver, the actuation, in turn, perturbs the diver’s movements. RADMCS integrates on the diver’s existing equipment by strapping onto the scuba diving air tank.
This eliminates the need for complex exoskeleton style suits or augmented swimming solutions that require additional training or instrumentation.
It is self-contained and does not interfere with the diver’s natural body movements beyond the perturbative feedback provided to the diver.

Our contributions are the

introduction of a novel, wearable underwater robotic system for movement assistance;

presentation of systematic testing of threshold sensitivity (TS) for haptic feedback with human participants in an underwater study in ocean and closed-water environments;

exploration of closed-water experiments of simulated wall-following to gauge the robot’s effectiveness at directing human participants;

demonstration of form, fit, and function of the current design in open-water field experiments for TS and maneuverability near coral reefs.

1. introduction of a novel, wearable underwater robotic system for movement assistance;

2. presentation of systematic testing of threshold sensitivity (TS) for haptic feedback with human participants in an underwater study in ocean and closed-water environments;

3. exploration of closed-water experiments of simulated wall-following to gauge the robot’s effectiveness at directing human participants;

4. demonstration of form, fit, and function of the current design in open-water field experiments for TS and maneuverability near coral reefs.

Construction remains one of the least automated sectors despite persistent labor shortages, hazardous working conditions, and declining productivity [ 1 ] . This stands in sharp contrast to more structured environments like manufacturing [ 2 ] and warehousing [ 3 ] , where advances in robotics and artificial intelligence have automated many core tasks. A key barrier to automating construction labor is the dynamic and unstructured nature of construction sites, requiring robots that navigate human-scale spaces and perform diverse tasks that combine mobility with dexterous manipulation [ 1 ] . Existing construction automation has largely focused on task-specific systems, either in controlled, off-site environments where robots perform repetitive tasks such as frame prefabrication [ 4 ] [ 5 ] , or directly on construction sites for activities such as bricklaying, drywall installation, and robotic assembly [ 5 ] [ 6 ] . This motivates the development of robotic systems that can operate directly in construction sites while supporting a broader range of tasks.

Humanoids are a natural fit for on-site construction work because their morphology matches spaces and tools built for humans. They can navigate tight spaces, climb steps, and use common tools without site modification or task-specific automation [ 1 ] . This allows humanoids to address a broad range of tasks with a single embodiment. While the existing humanoid models may not be optimal for every task, they offer reach and mobility no fixed or wheeled system can match [ 1 ] . However, reliable, full autonomy of humanoid robots remains out of the reach in the noise and clutter of a real construction site [ 1 ] .

Teleoperation offers a practical near-term path: skilled operators control robots remotely, reducing physical strain and hazard to workers while generating high-quality demonstration data for training future autonomous models. Manual material handling is a leading cause of musculoskeletal disorders [ 7 ] , and over 1,000 construction workers die on the job each year in the United States [ 8 ] . These conditions compound a persistent labor shortage, as an aging workforce approaches retirement while the physical toll deters new entrants [ 9 ] . By transferring strain and hazard to the robot while leveraging the operator’s expertise, teleoperation can improve worker safety and career longevity. Moreover, recent whole-body teleoperation systems have shown that expert demonstrations are key to training autonomous vision-language-action (VLA) models [ 10 , 11 , 12 ] . However, existing whole-body teleoperation systems have been evaluated primarily on household and laboratory tasks, leaving their effectiveness for construction work largely unexplored.

In this work, we develop a teleoperation system for a humanoid construction robot from a seated workstation using a Meta Quest 3 extended-reality (XR) headset for head and hand tracking and GLYDR foot pedals for locomotion control. We then deploy a Unitree G1 humanoid robot on an active construction site and perform a feasibility study on two high-importance construction tasks selected from the O*NET occupational database [ 13 ] . Our key contributions are:

A humanoid robot teleoperation system allowing seated, remote operation for simultaneous manipulation and locomotion built using open source components;

A humanoid robot teleoperation system allowing seated, remote operation for simultaneous manipulation and locomotion built using open source components;

Deployment on an active construction site;

A preliminary evaluation of teleoperation performance on two representative construction tasks.

1. A humanoid robot teleoperation system allowing seated, remote operation for simultaneous manipulation and locomotion built using open source components;

2. Deployment on an active construction site;

3. A preliminary evaluation of teleoperation performance on two representative construction tasks.

2026-10-05.introduction:
Robots plan and simulate on convex collision geometry. Sampling-based motion planners [ 1 , 2 ] , rigid-body simulators [ 3 , 4 , 5 , 6 ] , and contact-rich manipulation pipelines all rely on fast, robust proximity and penetration queries between convex bodies, computed with algorithms such as GJK and EPA [ 7 , 8 ] . Objects are therefore represented as convex decompositions , i.e., unions of convex parts that approximate a triangle mesh, and production simulators routinely build them with tools such as V-HACD [ 9 ] and CoACD [ 10 ] .

Image-to-3D generative models now offer a direct route from a camera image to 3D geometry. The strongest methods sample a high-quality shape latent with a large image-conditioned diffusion or flow-matching model in a single feed-forward pass [ 11 , 12 , 13 ] , and robotics increasingly uses them to build scene geometry for manipulation and planning [ 14 , 15 , 16 ] . However, these models are optimized for visual fidelity, not physical simulation . They output dense triangle meshes ( ∼ 10 6 {\sim}10^{6} faces) with small but near-ubiquitous topological defects such as non-manifold edges and stray fragments (quantified in § IV-A ). Turning such a mesh into collision geometry takes a fragile chain of decimation [ 17 ] , repair [ 18 ] , watertightening, and approximate convex decomposition. This reconstruct-then-decompose workflow [ 15 ] is slow and sensitive to errors at every stage. Skipping it is worse, because it does not fail loudly: as we show in § IV-B , physics engines accept the raw mesh and silently substitute a single convex hull, producing a plausible-looking scene with the wrong collision shape.

We propose I2CD ( I mage-to- C onvex- D ecomposition), which bypasses this workflow by predicting the convex decomposition directly from a single RGB image (Fig. 1 ). Our key insight is that the generator’s mesh is the wrong output but its features are the right input: a pretrained image-to-3D model already encodes the object’s shape, so only the readout needs to change. We therefore keep the image-conditioned diffusion transformer (DiT) and ShapeVAE decoder of Hunyuan3D-2 [ 11 ] frozen and replace the mesh readout with a lightweight cross-attention head. Its K K learned “convex-slot” tokens query the pretrained shape features, and each emits the H H halfplanes of one convex polytope in the CvxNet formulation [ 19 ] ; the union of the K K polytopes is the predicted shape. Only this head is trained, with a class-balanced occupancy loss and a convex-pair contrastive loss that suppresses the slab-stacking failure mode of CvxNet-style decoders.

I2CD introduces neither a new convex representation, which we inherit from CvxNet, nor a new 3D generator. Our contribution is to show, and validate for robotics, that a modern image-to-3D foundation model becomes a direct source of simulation geometry with a 38 M-parameter head and under ten GPU-hours of training, inheriting an open-world shape prior that, to our knowledge, no image-to-convex model trained from scratch currently offers. The single-view modes of CvxNet [ 19 ] and BSP-Net [ 20 ] are category-specific and have no open-world weights (§ II ). In summary, we:

adapt a frozen Hunyuan3D-2 backbone to emit K K halfplane-parameterized convexes directly, with a training objective that yields compact, spatially coherent parts;

benchmark against eight reconstruct-then-decompose pipelines (Hunyuan3D-2 and SAM3D [ 13 ] paired with CoACD, V-HACD, CvxNet, and BSP-Net) on 227 227 held-out OmniObject3D [ 21 ] and GSO [ 22 ] objects, where I2CD achieves the best volumetric IoU at 6 6 – 37 × 37\times lower end-to-end latency;

test simulation-readiness beyond “it loads” with a drop-and-settle study in MuJoCo, PyBullet, Genesis, and Isaac Sim (PhysX): raw generated meshes are silently replaced by a different shape or require costly preprocessing, while I2CD geometry is used as delivered in every engine;

execute 100 100 pick-and-place trials on a physical xArm7 in a 20 20 -object cluttered scene, where I2CD cuts the time to planner-ready scene geometry from 328 328 s to 11 11 s at comparable execution success ( 85 85 vs. 90 90 of 100 100 ).

1. adapt a frozen Hunyuan3D-2 backbone to emit K K halfplane-parameterized convexes directly, with a training objective that yields compact, spatially coherent parts;

2. benchmark against eight reconstruct-then-decompose pipelines (Hunyuan3D-2 and SAM3D [ 13 ] paired with CoACD, V-HACD, CvxNet, and BSP-Net) on 227 227 held-out OmniObject3D [ 21 ] and GSO [ 22 ] objects, where I2CD achieves the best volumetric IoU at 6 6 – 37 × 37\times lower end-to-end latency;

3. test simulation-readiness beyond “it loads” with a drop-and-settle study in MuJoCo, PyBullet, Genesis, and Isaac Sim (PhysX): raw generated meshes are silently replaced by a different shape or require costly preprocessing, while I2CD geometry is used as delivered in every engine;

4. execute 100 100 pick-and-place trials on a physical xArm7 in a 20 20 -object cluttered scene, where I2CD cuts the time to planner-ready scene geometry from 328 328 s to 11 11 s at comparable execution success ( 85 85 vs. 90 90 of 100 100 ).

2026-10-06.introduction:
Robotic grasping systems must remain reliable when object identity, pose, illumination, camera viewpoint, and hardware differ from the conditions represented during training. Although large public and synthetic datasets enable scalable end-to-end grasp learning, their visual statistics, sensor noise, robot embodiment, gripper geometry, and workspace configuration may not match a specific deployment. This domain gap can degrade grasp precision on the target system. Collecting a comparably large local dataset and retraining or fine-tuning a predictor for every installation is costly. The practical problem addressed in this work is therefore to achieve accurate grasping from only a small number of locally acquired and annotated examples.

RAGrasp addresses this problem through retrieval and adaptation, drawing inspiration from retrieval-augmented models that separate external memory from inference [ 1 ] . Rather than learning a task-specific grasp predictor from a public corpus, it builds a compact template bank from RGB-D observations collected with the deployment hardware. Given a new observation, the method isolates the query object, retrieves compatible local templates using geometric and semantic evidence, transfers the stored grasp through a confidence-gated dual-path estimator, and refines the result using mask-support and silhouette-contact constraints. A verified execution can optionally be stored as a new template. This design separates reusable grasp experience from the transfer procedure and expands deployment coverage without retraining the full system.

The main contributions are:

a data-efficient grasping formulation that uses a small, locally collected template bank instead of task-specific training on a large public grasp dataset;

a geometry-semantic retrieval cascade that combines scaled multi-start contour ICP with masked DINOv2 ranking;

a confidence-gated dual transfer mechanism that chooses between contour alignment and feature-correspondence-based similarity estimation;

a mask-constrained local refinement stage followed by calibrated metric execution and success-conditioned memory write-back; and

a real-world evaluation across seen and unseen objects, viewpoint and illumination changes, template-bank sizes, similarity strata, and component ablations.

1. a data-efficient grasping formulation that uses a small, locally collected template bank instead of task-specific training on a large public grasp dataset;

2. a geometry-semantic retrieval cascade that combines scaled multi-start contour ICP with masked DINOv2 ranking;

3. a confidence-gated dual transfer mechanism that chooses between contour alignment and feature-correspondence-based similarity estimation;

4. a mask-constrained local refinement stage followed by calibrated metric execution and success-conditioned memory write-back; and

5. a real-world evaluation across seen and unseen objects, viewpoint and illumination changes, template-bank sizes, similarity strata, and component ablations.
